        Suppose we have budget B.  If we choose a set S with c_i = 1, cost = sum_{i in S} p_i.  Total items = |S| + sum_j |{ i in S : i != j, f_i | f_j }|.  If we have budget left, we can buy more copies.  Buying a copy of item k (whether in S or not) costs p_k and adds 1 to total.  If k not in S, it might also add free copies.  But if we already have S optimized, any additional copy of an item not in S would cost at least the minimum price among items not in S, and might add some free copies.  However, we could also just buy copies of the item in S with the smallest price, getting +1 per copy.  Since free copies are just +1 each, and buying a new item costs at least min_price, and might give at most (number of j it divides) free copies, which could be more than 1.  But we have to pay its price.  It's a knapsack-like choice.

