        What if outdeg(i) is large and we have many items?  The grouping by price will have at most n distinct prices.  The number of iterations is at most the number of distinct prices < 2*p_min, which is <= n.  This is O(n log n) for sorting and O(n) for the loop.  Computing outdeg(i) for all i: we have n items, factors f_i <= n.  We can compute outdeg(i) efficiently.  Since factor_i <= n, we can use a sieve-like approach.  For each factor f, we have a list of items with that factor.  But outdeg(i) is the number of j != i such that f_i | f_j.  We can compute for each factor f, the number of items with factor f, and also which factors are multiples of f.  Since f_i <= n, we can create an array count_factor[f] = number of items with factor f.  Also, for each item i, outdeg(i) = sum_{g multiple of f_i, g != f_i} count_factor[g]?  Wait, outdeg(i) is the number of j != i with f_i | f_j.  If there are multiple items with the same factor f_j, each counts.  Also, we must exclude j = i.  So outdeg(i) = (number of items j with f_i | f_j and j != i).  This can be computed as: for each factor f, let total_items_with_factor[f] = number of items with factor f.  Then for an item i with factor f_i, the number of j (including i itself) with f_i | f_j is sum_{g: f_i | g} total_items_with_factor[g].  Then outdeg(i) = that sum minus 1 (for j=i) if f_i divides f_i?  But f_i always divides f_i.  So if we sum over all g divisible by f_i, we include the item i itself.  So outdeg(i) = (sum_{g divisible by f_i} total_items_with_factor[g]) - 1.  But careful: if there are multiple items with factor f_i, then the sum includes all of them, and we subtract 1 for the specific item i.  This is correct because outdeg(i) counts all j != i with f_i | f_j.  Since f_i | f_i, the sum includes all items with factor f_i, including i.  Subtracting 1 removes i.  What about j with f_j divisible by f_i but f_j != f_i? They are included.  So outdeg(i) = (sum_{g: f_i | g} total_items_with_factor[g]) - 1.  This is O(n) per item if we do it naively, but we can precompute for each factor f the sum of total_items_with_factor over its multiples.  Since factor <= n, we can do a sieve: for f from 1 to n, for multiple m = f, 2f, 3f, ... <= n, add total_items_with_factor[m] to a array sum_multiples[f].  Then outdeg(i) = sum_multiples[f_i] - 1.  This is O(n log n) because sum_{f=1}^n n/f = O(n log n).  n <= 1e5, so this is very fast.

