        Now, what about the -1 in outdeg formula?  The formula outdeg(i) = sum_multiples[f_i] - 1 assumes that the item i itself is included in the sum_multiples.  Since f_i always divides f_i, the sum_multiples includes all items with factor f_i, including i.  Subtracting 1 removes i.  But what if there are multiple items with factor f_i?  The sum includes all of them.  Subtracting 1 removes only the specific item i.  The remaining items with factor f_i are still counted.  This correctly gives the number of j != i with f_i | f_j, which includes all other items with factor f_i and all items with factors that are proper multiples.  This is exactly what we need.

