        It seems buying the cheapest first is always optimal because the value per cost is higher (or equal) for cheaper premium copies.  Since all premium copies give the same value (2), and we have a filler with value 1 per p_min, the cheapest premium copies are the most "efficient" in terms of minimizing cost for a given number of premium copies.  And since the filler term is floor((B - cost)/p_min), minimizing cost for a given number of premium copies maximizes the filler value.  And because we can always choose to buy fewer premium copies and more filler, the optimal is to buy as many premium copies as possible starting from the cheapest.  This is a standard result for this type of knapsack where all premium items have the same value and we have a linear filler.

